      "name": "Pretreatment renal function",
      "members": [
        "Serum creatinine"
      ],
      "interpretation": "Serum creatinine is a distinct pretreatment renal-function measurement. Its main-effect pattern is clinically plausible and fairly internally consistent for prediction: it was retained as an outcome predictor in all 3 penalized outcome analyses and as a treatment-selection predictor in 2 of 3 penalized treatment analyses. Evidence for treatment-effect heterogeneity is weaker and less consistent: only 1 of 3 penalized interaction analyses selected it, 2 of 3 univariable interaction screens gave nominal p-values below 0.05, but all 3 univariable R-learner analyses failed to show validation R-loss gain and the causal forest showed only 1 positive permutation R-loss increase out of 3. Overall, creatinine looks more like a prognostic and treatment-selection variable than a robust treatment-effect modifier in these overlapping analyses.",
      "disagreements": "The univariable interaction screen suggests some effect modification in 2 of 3 analyses, with p-values 0.02, 0.04, and 0.20, but this is not corroborated by the univariable R-learner: validation R-loss gains were -0.002, 0.000, and -0.001, none above 0. Penalized interaction was selected in only 1 of 3 analyses, and the causal forest gave positive permutation R-loss increases in only 1 of 3 analyses, with values -0.001, 0.001, and 0.000. Therefore, the interaction evidence is inconsistent and likely weak."
    },
    {
      "name": "Pretreatment pulmonary structural disease",
      "members": [
        "Emphysema on imaging"
      ],
      "interpretation": "Emphysema on imaging is a separate pulmonary structural-disease measurement. Its effect-modification signal is more consistent than that of serum creatinine. It was selected in all 3 penalized treatment-interaction analyses, all 3 causal-forest permutation analyses, and all 3 univariable R-learner analyses had positive validation R-loss gains, although the gains were modest, approximately 0.004, 0.006, and 0.003. Its prognostic main effect is less consistent, with only 1 of 3 penalized outcome analyses selecting it, and whether it influenced treatment selection is unknown because penalized treatment main-effect results were unavailable. These overlapping analyses support imaging evidence of emphysema as a candidate treatment-effect modifier, while leaving its role in treatment choice and its prognostic importance open.",
      "disagreements": "The effect-modification findings are mostly concordant, but not perfectly: univariable interaction p-values met nominal significance in 2 of 3 analyses, with values 0.03, 0.06, and 0.04, whereas penalized interaction selection was 3 of 3 and causal-forest positive permutation R-loss increases were also 3 of 3. The missing penalized treatment main-effect results leave uncertainty about treatment-selection associations, and the R-loss gains, though consistently positive, are small, limiting certainty about clinical magnitude."
    }
  ]
